==English==

===Etymology===
Coined by {{w|William Shakespeare}}.<ref>{{RQ:Shakespeare Antony|act=I|scene=v|page=344|passage=''Cleo.'' My Sallad dayes, / When I was greene in iudgment, cold in blood}}</ref><ref>{{cite-book|author=Albert Jack|year=2005|title=Red herrings and white elephants|publisher=HarperCollins|page= 44|pageurl=http://books.google.co.jp/books?id=NSMq-PhYVq4C&pg=PA44|ISBN=978-0-06-084337-3|passage=The phrase is a simple one with a simple origin provided, once again, by Shakespeare.}}</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-plural noun|head=[[salad]] [[day]]s}}

# A [[period]] of [[inexperienced]] [[youthful]] [[innocence]] accompanied by [[enthusiasm]] and [[idealism]].
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|chapter=XX|passage=“I'll bet he was swiping things as a small boy.” “Only biscuits.” “I beg your pardon?” “Or crackers you would call them, wouldn't you? He was telling me he occasionally pinched a cracker or two in his '''salad days'''.”}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2015|actor=Timothy Dalton|role=Sir Malcolm Murray|title=Penny Dreadful|season=2|number=6|time=23m30s|passage=Do you know I've not been to a ball in ages? I used to be quite the dancer in my '''salad days''', if you can believe such a thing.}}

